{"t":1793131546,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"ec20ce0a-4d95-437c-b254-335d37608195","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-27T19:23:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 271923\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n323 PM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo changes to the forecast.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Benign weather continues through the remainder of October\n  with near to above normal temperatures expected. \n \n- 2) On-off rain showers are possible beginning Sunday into\n  early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Benign weather continues through the remainder \nof October with near to above normal temperatures expected. \n\nA weak front to our south will retreat northward as a warm front\nWednesday morning. A large area of high pressure will build to \nthe north Thursday through Saturday. As a result, expect passing\nhigh clouds and a series of wind shifts with each of the \nfrontal passages. Winds will change to the south and southwest \nlate Wednesday through Friday. This will yield seasonable to \nabove normal temperatures for the remainder of the workweek. \nHighs will generally range from the mid 60s and low 70s though \nWednesday with low to mid 70s Thursday and mid to upper 70s \nFriday. This is in part to the position of the high to the \nnorth leading to increased southeast to southwest winds across \nthe region. Overnight lows will remain mild as well in the mid \n40s and low 50s.\n\nThere is the potential for some low clouds, fog, or even a\nlittle drizzle during the late night into morning hours each of\nthe next few days as onshore flow develops.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...On-off rain showers are possible beginning Sunday\ninto early next week.\n\nSurface high pressure lingers on Friday before tracking north of the \nregion on Saturday, with generally sunny & warm conditions \npersisting through then. Highs on Friday could reach into the mid-to-\nupper 70s across the metros as this persistent high pressure has \nallowed for temperatures to gradually build throughout the week.\n\nMeanwhile, a broad area of low pressure will form over the Midwest \nand then track east late in the weekend, bringing increasing cloud \ncover to the region throughout the day Saturday. A stemming cold \nfront will cause temperatures to drop Saturday as this \nsystem tracks through the Mid-Atlantic, with potential rain showers \nbeginning on Sunday. Uncertainty remains high on total spread and \nrainfall accumulation as the system looks somewhat disorganized in \nmodel guidance. Regardless, this will be the first chance for rain \nin well over a week across the region. While temperatures drop back \ninto the upper 60s Saturday & Sunday, this remains somewhat above \naverage for the start of November. \n\nAs this system tracks offshore by Tuesday, a second, potentially \nmore potent upper-level low could form over the Plains and make its \nway to the region by the middle of next week. Uncertainty remains \nhigh regarding the track of this system, but this could bring \nadditional rainfall to the region as we get into November.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /19Z TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected through tonight. Winds will remain \nlight out of the north to northeast. Light onshore flow could \nlead to some patchy low clouds/fog in outlying areas late \ntonight.\n\nWinds will shift to the southeast Wednesday ahead of a warm \nfrontal boundary. This will lead to the potential for low \nclouds and fog, especially at CHO and MRB given the marine \ninfluence/increased surface moisture banking along the eastern \nside of the Blue Ridge mountains and up the Potomac River Valley.\nConfidence in this is low, although hi-res models have shown \nsome consistency in recent cycles. Aside from early morning low\nclouds and/or fog, VFR conditions are expected Wednesday night \nthrough Friday as high pressure builds along the coast. Winds \nwill turn south to southwesterly Thursday.\n\nVFR conditions are anticipated to persist through Saturday. \nBeginning Sunday, a passing cold front could bring rain showers and \nlowering cloud decks that could bring temporary sub-VFR conditions \nto terminals. Winds generally stay under 10 knots, flowing \nnorthwesterly Saturday before shifting southeasterly on Sunday in \nthe wake of the front. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nLight northeast to east winds expected through tonight. Winds \nbecome southeasterly Wednesday before turning southerly Thursday.\nSome channeling is possible at times, though overall gradients \nlook weak. Winds stay below SCA thresholds through the end of \nthe month. \n\nWinds remain below SCA thresholds through the weekend. Southerly \nwinds shift northwesterly on Friday, then southeasterly by Sunday in \nthe wake of a cold front.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nLight onshore flow Wednesday into Thursday may push sensitive \nshoreline close to minor flood stage. Areas most at risk for\nreaching minor tidal flood thresholds would be places like \nAnnapolis and Alexandria mainly during the evening tide cycles \n(which are astronomically higher).\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...KLW/DHOF/EST/SRT\nAVIATION...KLW/DHOF/EST/SRT\nMARINE...KLW/DHOF/EST/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1793131493,"days":{"2026-10-27":{"hi":70,"lo":50,"pop":2,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-28":{"hi":69,"lo":51,"pop":2,"short":"Patchy Fog then Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-29":{"hi":73,"lo":52,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-30":{"hi":76,"lo":50,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-31":{"hi":68,"lo":50,"pop":3,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-01":{"hi":69,"lo":56,"pop":41,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-11-02":{"hi":70,"lo":56,"pop":41,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1793131200,"n":48,"ceil